## TODO

Goal: replace the command-line output with a Flask website.

### Flask web app
- [ ] Add a `app.py` with Flask and a basic page layout (templates + CSS, e.g. Bootstrap or Tailwind)
- [ ] Refactor `main.py` so each step **returns data instead of printing** (roster VORP rows, drop/add moves, trade ideas as dicts/dataclasses) so both the CLI and the website can use it
- [ ] Pages: login/connect, league picker, roster + VORP table, drop/add suggestions, trade finder
- [ ] Sliders/inputs on the trade page for `MIN_MY_GAIN`, `THEIR_TOLERANCE`, `MIN_VALUE_RATIO` so users can tune fairness without editing code
- [ ] Loading/progress indicator while the model trains

### Login on a website (the hard part)
- [ ] The Selenium browser login only works when the app runs **on the user's own computer**. Decide: local-only app, or hosted multi-user site?
- [ ] For a hosted site, replace Selenium with another way to get cookies (e.g. user pastes `SWID` + `espn_s2`, a browser extension, or a bookmarklet)
- [ ] Store each user's credentials **encrypted** (not a plain JSON file), tied to a user session; never log them
- [ ] Add user accounts/sessions and a "disconnect and delete my data" button
- [ ] Handle expired cookies gracefully in the UI (prompt to reconnect instead of re-opening a browser)

### Performance and architecture
- [ ] Run training in a background job (Celery/RQ or a thread) so requests don't time out
- [ ] Cache league data and save trained models to disk instead of retraining every run
- [ ] Add rate limiting / caching for ESPN requests to avoid being blocked
- [ ] Replace `print` with the `logging` module

### Modeling improvements
- [ ] Support IDP leagues
- [ ] Better K and D/ST predictions
- [ ] Model bye weeks and playoff weeks properly
- [ ] Handle roster-size limits (forced drops) in trade search
- [ ] Waiver priority / FAAB budget awareness
- [ ] Hyperparameter tuning and feature importance reporting
- [ ] Estimate how likely each opponent is to accept a trade

### Project quality
- [ ] Add automated tests (`pytest`) for lineup, VORP, trades, and feature building
- [ ] Add type hints and docstrings throughout
- [ ] Choose and add a license
- [ ] Add screenshots to this README once the website exists